{"type": "brief", "t": 1791243218.185, "patient": "SYN-Y", "markdown": "# Pre-visit brief - Patient Y (SYN-Y)\n> SYNTHETIC RECORD - invented for a demo, not a real patient. For clinician review; not medical advice.\n\n## Snapshot\nJordan Ellis is a 72-year-old woman with HFrEF with EF 30%, CAD with prior inferior MI, type 2 diabetes, CKD stage 3a, and limited transport while living alone [SQL:patient] [SQL:condition]. She is coming after a 2026-09-04 discharge for her second HF readmission in 90 days, with recurrent congestion symptoms reported by phone on 2026-09-15 [SQL:encounter] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013]. The immediate concerns are recurrent volume overload driven by diuretic nonadherence/barriers, hyperkalemia with potassium chloride plus lisinopril plus spironolactone, and rapid eGFR decline with rising BNP [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] [SQL:lab_result].\n\n## What changed\n- **Recurrent congestion after discharge.** She was discharged on 2026-09-04, but by the 2026-09-15 nurse call her weight was 73.9 kg, which was 3.1 kg above discharge weight, with new ankle swelling and two-pillow orthopnea [SQL:vital_sign] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013]. BNP has risen across admissions from 980 on 2026-06-03 to 1240 on 2026-07-19 to 1410 on 2026-08-30 [SQL:lab_result].\n- **Readmissions appear barrier-driven.** The 2026-07-19 readmission occurred 41 days after discharge with orthopnea after she stopped the afternoon furosemide dose to avoid frequent urination while taking the bus [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009]. The pharmacy note on 2026-07-30 documented that the furosemide refill was picked up 9 days late and that medication copays were difficult on a fixed income [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010].\n- **Hyperkalemia and discharge medication mismatch.** Potassium increased from 4.6 mmol/L on 2026-06-03 to 4.9 mmol/L on 2026-07-19 to 5.6 mmol/L on 2026-08-30 [SQL:lab_result]. Potassium chloride was held during the 2026-08-30 hospitalization because of hyperkalemia while she was on spironolactone, lisinopril, and potassium chloride, but potassium chloride appeared again on the discharge medication list [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012].\n- **Kidney function is worsening quickly.** eGFR fell from 51 on 2026-06-03 to 46 on 2026-07-19 to 42 mL/min/1.73m2 on 2026-08-30 [SQL:lab_result]. Her eGFR slope is -37.37 mL/min/1.73m2 per year, which is much more negative and therefore a faster decline than the type 2 diabetes cohort 10th percentile of -5.88 mL/min/1.73m2 per year [SQL:lab_result] [SQL:cohort_benchmark].\n- **Transition supports remain incomplete.** A home scale was provided after the 2026-07-24 discharge, but home health nursing was declined because of cost after the 2026-09-04 discharge [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012]. She asked for transport assistance for the clinic visit on 2026-09-15, and a transport assistance referral was requested [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013] [SQL:referral].\n\n## Medications to review\n| Medication | Concern | What the chart shows | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Potassium chloride 20 mEq once daily | hyperkalemia with CKD and RAAS/MRA therapy | This medication was active after starting 2026-06-08, and potassium was 5.6 mmol/L on 2026-08-30 [SQL:medication] [SQL:lab_result]. It was held during the 2026-08-30 hospitalization for hyperkalemia while on spironolactone, lisinopril, and potassium chloride, but it reappeared on the discharge medication list [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012]. | [SQL:medication] [SQL:lab_result] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012] |\n| Furosemide 40 mg twice daily | nonadherence from urinary burden and access barriers | She stopped the afternoon dose before the 2026-07-19 readmission to avoid urinating frequently while taking the bus [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009]. The furosemide refill was picked up 9 days late, and she reported difficulty with copays [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010]. | [SQL:medication] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010] |\n| Spironolactone 25 mg once daily | hyperkalemia risk as eGFR declines | Spironolactone is active with lisinopril and potassium chloride, and potassium rose to 5.6 mmol/L with eGFR 42 on 2026-08-30 [SQL:medication] [SQL:lab_result]. The inpatient note identified the combination of spironolactone, lisinopril, and potassium chloride during the hyperkalemia episode [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011]. | [SQL:medication] [SQL:lab_result] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] |\n| Lisinopril 10 mg once daily | renal and potassium monitoring needed | Lisinopril is active while eGFR is falling rapidly and potassium is elevated [SQL:medication] [SQL:lab_result]. The latest systolic BP was 108 mmHg on 2026-09-04, which may limit rapid HF medication titration [SQL:vital_sign]. | [SQL:medication] [SQL:lab_result] [SQL:vital_sign] |\n| Carvedilol 6.25 mg twice daily | HFrEF therapy present but low-normal BP may limit titration | Carvedilol is active for HFrEF, and the latest systolic BP was 108 mmHg on 2026-09-04 [SQL:medication] [SQL:vital_sign]. Recurrent congestion and rising BNP suggest the immediate problem is decompensation and adherence rather than absence of beta-blocker therapy [SQL:lab_result] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013]. | [SQL:medication] [SQL:lab_result] [SQL:vital_sign] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013] |\n| Metformin 500 mg twice daily | renal function trend should be watched | Metformin is active for type 2 diabetes, and eGFR declined to 42 mL/min/1.73m2 on 2026-08-30 [SQL:medication] [SQL:lab_result]. The rapid eGFR slope makes follow-up renal monitoring important [SQL:lab_result] [SQL:cohort_benchmark]. | [SQL:medication] [SQL:lab_result] [SQL:cohort_benchmark] |\n\nThe highest-risk medication interaction is potassium chloride plus lisinopril plus spironolactone in CKD with documented potassium 5.6 mmol/L, while diuretic nonadherence is likely worsening congestion and readmission risk [SQL:medication] [SQL:lab_result] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011].\n\n## Overdue monitoring and open care gaps\n- **Active congestion symptoms not yet reassessed in clinic.** On 2026-09-15 she reported new ankle swelling and needing two pillows to sleep, with weight 3.1 kg above discharge [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013]. These symptoms followed two HF readmissions within 90 days [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011].\n- **Potassium chloride safety issue unresolved.** Potassium chloride was held during the 2026-08-30 hospitalization because potassium was high, but the discharge medication list again included potassium chloride [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012]. Current active medications include potassium chloride 20 mEq daily with lisinopril and spironolactone [SQL:medication].\n- **No SGLT2 inhibitor documented.** No SGLT2 inhibitor was on the discharge medication list, and the reason was not documented [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012]. This is an open HFrEF and diabetes/CKD medication optimization question once potassium, renal function, BP, cost, and access are reviewed [SQL:condition] [SQL:lab_result].\n- **Home health support declined because of cost.** Home health nursing was declined because of cost after discharge [SQL:referral] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012]. This matters because readmissions have been linked in the chart to medication barriers, late refill, transport, and difficulty managing diuretic timing [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013].\n- **Transport assistance pending.** She asked about transport assistance for the clinic visit during the 2026-09-15 call [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013]. A referral for transport assistance was requested on 2026-09-15 [SQL:referral].\n\n## What the reference and research say\n### Clinical reference\n- The kidney disease reference states that keeping the proper blood potassium level is essential and that impaired kidneys can allow potassium to build up in the blood, causing severe heart problems [MEDQUAD-01695#chunk-0]. It also describes dietary management as part of preventing potassium buildup in kidney disease [MEDQUAD-01695#chunk-1]. *This supports prioritizing potassium safety education and medication reconciliation because Patient Y has CKD stage 3a and potassium 5.6 mmol/L while potassium chloride, lisinopril, and spironolactone are listed [SQL:condition] [SQL:lab_result] [SQL:medication].*\n- The heart failure symptom reference describes shortness of breath, fatigue, swelling, and weight gain as signs related to fluid buildup [MEDQUAD-03643#chunk-0]. Another heart failure reference states that shortness of breath, tiredness, and swelling are frequent signs caused by fluid accumulation [MEDQUAD-04254#chunk-0]. *This matches her post-discharge ankle swelling, two-pillow orthopnea, and 3.1 kg weight gain, so her symptoms should be treated as possible recurrent congestion [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013].*\n- The heart failure prevention reference lists lifestyle steps including reducing salt and fluid intake and working toward a healthy weight [MEDQUAD-03656#chunk-0]. *This supports reinforcing a concrete low-salt plan, fluid instructions if prescribed, and daily weight response plan at the visit [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013].*\n- The MEDQUAD search did not return useful specific threshold guidance for MRA potassium/eGFR cutoffs, RAAS/MRA lab monitoring intervals, SGLT2 inhibitor indications, or transport/cost transition interventions. *The clinician should use local HF protocols for thresholds and monitoring while still acting on the chart-documented hyperkalemia and recurrent congestion [SQL:lab_result] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011].*\n\n### Research evidence\n- A PubMed-indexed study of HF signs and symptoms reported that hospitalized patients had more sudden weight gain, and sudden weight gain increased from 5% in functional class I to 37% in functional class IV [PMID-19940253]. The study supports asking specifically about weight gain as a marker of worsening HF status [PMID-19940253]. *This is directly relevant because Patient Y is 3.1 kg above discharge weight with ankle swelling and orthopnea after two recent admissions [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011].*\n- The PubMed search returned titles on HF disease management programs, primary care intensity, and exercise adherence, but the evidence-researcher returned only one usable abstract finding with a PMID for this run [PMID-19940253]. *The available evidence finding still supports treating weight gain as a serious readmission warning sign, while transition interventions should be driven by chart barriers and local care pathways [PMID-19940253] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010].*\n- The evidence-researcher did not return usable cited PubMed findings for SGLT2 inhibitor trials, hyperkalemia risk quantification with MRA plus ACE inhibitor in CKD/HF, or transport/cost interventions in this run. *Those remain important clinical questions for the clinician, but they are not cited here beyond the chart gap that no SGLT2 inhibitor reason was documented [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012].*\n\n## Insights for the clinician: next steps\n1. **Assess congestion and need for urgent escalation first.** She is 3.1 kg above discharge weight with new ankle swelling and two-pillow orthopnea after two 90-day HF readmissions, and HF references identify swelling, shortness of breath, and weight gain as fluid-buildup symptoms [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] [MEDQUAD-03643#chunk-0] [MEDQUAD-04254#chunk-0]. Rising BNP from 980 to 1240 to 1410 pg/mL strengthens concern for recurrent decompensation [SQL:lab_result].\n2. **Obtain same-day BMP and reconcile potassium immediately.** Potassium was 5.6 mmol/L with eGFR 42, and potassium chloride reappeared on the discharge list after being held inpatient for hyperkalemia while on lisinopril and spironolactone [SQL:lab_result] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012]. Kidney disease reference warns that impaired kidneys can allow potassium buildup that can cause severe heart problems [MEDQUAD-01695#chunk-0].\n3. **Medication change awaits doctor: stop potassium chloride if confirmed active.** Medication-safety resolved escalation 33 by proposing action 221 to stop potassium chloride because of hyperkalemia risk with lisinopril, spironolactone, and CKD, and this medication change needs the physician decision under policy CP-02 [SQL:medication] [SQL:lab_result] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011].\n4. **Address the readmission driver: diuretic timing, adherence, copays, and transport.** Her July readmission followed stopping afternoon furosemide to avoid urination on the bus, and pharmacy later documented a furosemide refill picked up 9 days late with copay difficulty [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010]. Solving dose timing, bathroom access, refill synchronization, copay support, and transport is likely as important as changing doses [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013] [SQL:referral].\n5. **Review GDMT after stabilization, including SGLT2 inhibitor gap.** No SGLT2 inhibitor was on the discharge medication list and no reason was documented [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012]. Because she has HFrEF, diabetes, CKD, low-normal BP, rapid eGFR decline, and cost barriers, the clinician should document eligibility or a specific reason to defer after acute congestion and potassium are addressed [SQL:condition] [SQL:lab_result] [SQL:vital_sign] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010].\n6. **Teach a concrete daily weight and symptom action plan.** HF references connect weight gain, swelling, and shortness of breath to fluid buildup, and prevention advice includes salt/fluid reduction and weight management [MEDQUAD-03643#chunk-0] [MEDQUAD-04254#chunk-0] [MEDQUAD-03656#chunk-0]. PubMed evidence also supports sudden weight gain as a marker seen more often in severe and hospitalized HF patients [PMID-19940253].\n\n## Proposed actions (awaiting clinician approval)\n- **#217 \u00b7 lab request.** Order a basic metabolic panel soon to recheck potassium and renal function after hyperkalemia and rapid eGFR decline.\n- **#220 \u00b7 patient message.** Send the proposed patient message labeled \u201cImportant update about your heart condition\u201d to reinforce urgent symptom, medication, and follow-up instructions.\n- **#219 \u00b7 follow up.** Schedule heart failure clinic follow-up within 14 days after discharge, with transport needs addressed.\n- **#221 \u00b7 medication change.** Stop potassium chloride due to hyperkalemia risk with lisinopril, spironolactone, and CKD; this awaits the doctor under policy CP-02.\n\n## Questions for the visit\n1. Since discharge, how many furosemide doses has she missed, and is the afternoon dose still avoided because of bus travel or urinary urgency?\n2. What was her exact discharge weight, and what home weights has she recorded each day since 2026-09-04?\n3. Is she currently taking potassium chloride, spironolactone, and lisinopril together, and did anyone tell her to restart potassium after it was held in the hospital?\n4. Does she have worsening dyspnea at rest, chest pain, presyncope, confusion, decreased urine output, or inability to lie flat that would require same-day acute evaluation?\n5. Can she afford furosemide and other HF medications this month, and would pharmacy synchronization, copay assistance, mail delivery, or blister packs solve the late refill problem?\n6. What transport plan will get her to near-term HF follow-up and lab testing?\n7. Why was no SGLT2 inhibitor prescribed at discharge, and are renal function, BP, cost, or other contraindications preventing initiation?\n\n## Sources\n- [SQL:patient] Patient demographics\n- [SQL:condition] Diagnoses and social determinants\n- [SQL:encounter] Hospitalization and discharge timeline\n- [SQL:lab_result] Laboratory results and calculated eGFR slope\n- [SQL:cohort_benchmark] Type 2 diabetes cohort eGFR slope benchmark\n- [SQL:vital_sign] Weights and blood pressure measurements\n- [SQL:medication] Active and recent medications\n- [SQL:referral] Referral status including home health and transport assistance\n- [SYN-Y-NOTE-0008] 2026-06-03 HF admission and discharge note\n- [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] 2026-07-19 HF readmission note\n- [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010] 2026-07-30 pharmacy note\n- [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] 2026-08-30 HF readmission note\n- [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012] 2026-09-04 discharge summary\n- [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013] 2026-09-15 nurse telephone call\n- [MEDQUAD-01695#chunk-0] What to do for Diabetic Kidney Disease ?\n- [MEDQUAD-01695#chunk-1] What are the symptoms of Diabetic Kidney Disease ?\n- [MEDQUAD-03643#chunk-0] What are the symptoms of Heart Failure ?\n- [MEDQUAD-04254#chunk-0] What is (are) Heart Failure ?\n- [MEDQUAD-03656#chunk-0] How to prevent Heart Failure ?\n- [PMID-19940253] Signs and symptoms of heart failure: are you asking the right questions?\n", "accepted": true}
