{"type": "tool_result", "t": 1791243647.864, "agent": "medication-safety", "name": "search_clinical_reference", "preview": "Found 6 hybrid matches from 'clinical_reference':\nIMPORTANT: Cite Doc IDs when using this information.\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-05638#chunk-1] (relevance: 0.032)\n  Title: What is (are) hyperkalemic periodic paralysis ?\n  Content: Most people with hyperkalemic periodic paralysis have increased levels of potassium in their blood (hyperkalemia) during attacks. Hyperkalemia results when the weak or paralyzed muscles release potassium ions into the bloodstream. In other cases, attacks are associated with normal blood potassium levels (normokalemia). Ingesting potassium can trigger attacks in affected individuals, even if blood potassium levels do not go up.\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-01416#chunk-0] (relevance: 0.030)\n  Title: What to do for Anemia in Chronic Kidney Disease ?\n  Content: Question: What to do for Anemia in Chronic Kidney Disease ?\nAnswer:-Anemia is a condition in which the body has fewer red blood cells than normal. Red blood cells carry oxygen to tissues and organs throughout the body and enable them to use energy from food.-Anemia commonly occurs in people with chronic kidney disease (CKD)the permanent, partial loss of kidney function. Most people who have total loss of kidney function, or kidney failure, have anemia.-When kidneys are diseased or damaged, they ...\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-01409#chunk-0] (relevance: 0.016)\n  Title: What is (are) Anemia in Chronic Kidney Disease ?\n  Content: Question: What is (are) Anemia in Chronic Kidney Disease ?\nAnswer: The kidneys are two bean-shaped organs, each about the size of a fist. They are located just below the rib cage, one on each side of the spine. Every day, the kidneys filter about 120 to 150 quarts of blood to produce about 1 to 2 quarts of urine.\n\nHealthy kidneys produce a hormone called erythropoietin (EPO). A hormone is a chemical produced by the body and released into the blood to help trigger or regulate particular body func...\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-01697#chunk-0] (relevance: 0.016)\n  Title: What to do for Nutrition for Advanced Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?\n  Content: Question: What to do for Nutrition for Advanced Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?\nAnswer:-A person may prevent or delay some health problems from chronic kidney disease (CKD) by eating the right foods and avoiding foods high in sodium, potassium, and phosphorus.-The kidneys remove wastes and extra water from the blood and make urine.-Medical nutrition therapy (MNT) is the use of counseling by a registered dietitian to help promote a medical or health goal.-Dietitians who specialize in helping p...\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-03839#chunk-3] (relevance: 0.016)\n  Title: Who is at risk for Osteoporosis? ?\n  Content: ____Im not active.____I have one of these medical conditions:-Hyperthyroidism-Chronic lung disease-Cancer-Inflammatory bowel disease-Chronic hepatic or renal disease-Hyperparathyroidism-Vitamin D deficiency-Cushings disease-Multiple sclerosis-Rheumatoid arthritis-Hyperthyroidism-Chronic lung disease-Cancer-Inflammatory bowel disease-Chro ..."}
{"type": "thought", "t": 1791243657.263, "agent": "medication-safety", "text": "Escalation ID 34:\nDecision: Accepted.\nCare Action: #226 (newly proposed)\nStatus: needs_physician (under policy CP-02)\n\nI propose to hold Spironolactone for patient SYN-Y. The patient has hyperkalemia (Potassium 5.6 mmol/L on 2026-08-30 [SQL:lab_result]) and declining renal function (eGFR 42 mL/min/1.73m2 on 2026-08-30 [SQL:lab_result]). Spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic, and its continued use in the presence of hyperkalemia and declining renal function increases the risk of severe hyperkalemia. Holding spironolactone is a safer initial step to manage the patient's hyperkalemia and declining renal function. Chronic Kidney Disease can allow potassium buildup causing serious heart problems [MEDQUAD-01695#chunk-0]."}
